00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:02.110
And we'll going to have
other microservices that are

00:22:02.110 --> 00:22:03.276
going to talk to each other.

00:22:03.276 --> 00:22:05.440
As we add more
users to the system,

00:22:05.440 --> 00:22:07.330
they lose the system
with more traffic,

00:22:07.330 --> 00:22:10.650
we're going to see more
people hitting the surface.

00:22:10.650 --> 00:22:13.690
And we're going to want
to know how requests flow

00:22:13.690 --> 00:22:15.370
from one node to
another in the system,

00:22:15.370 --> 00:22:17.520
how they move from one
microservice to another.

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:19.132
And this is a very
common use case,

00:22:19.132 --> 00:22:21.340
especially as you start to
conduct more and more data

00:22:21.340 --> 00:22:22.846
over the wire using
microservices.

00:22:22.846 --> 00:22:24.220
You need to be
able to understand

00:22:24.220 --> 00:22:25.360
where something happened.

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:28.570
You've heard it said before
that moving to microservices

00:22:28.570 --> 00:22:31.540
is like making every production
incident a murder mystery.

00:22:31.540 --> 00:22:33.040
We have to figure
out what happened.

00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:34.360
We need to understand
where latency is.

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:35.530
We need to find out
where there's a problem.

00:22:35.530 --> 00:22:38.140
And then one way to do this
is to use distributed tracing

00:22:38.140 --> 00:22:43.000
as popularized fittingly by
the Dapper Paper published

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:44.816
by Google back in 2011.

00:22:44.816 --> 00:22:46.690
Distributed chasing in
theory is very simple.

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:49.000
For every request that
enters or exits a system,

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:50.980
at every ingress or
egress in the system,

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:53.440
we have a unique ID that
gets added to the message

00:22:53.440 --> 00:22:54.696
if it's not there already.

00:22:54.696 --> 00:22:56.320
And it gets perpetuated
if it is there.

00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:58.360
So we take a message,
look at the unique ID,

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:00.860
and then we make sure that we
send another outgoing message,

00:23:00.860 --> 00:23:01.930
we add that same ID.

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:04.300
That correlation ID
allows us to then

00:23:04.300 --> 00:23:06.710
record the receipt
of that message

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:09.520
and then see where that
message has been later on.

00:23:09.520 --> 00:23:11.230
We can publish
information about where

00:23:11.230 --> 00:23:12.340
that message has been later on.

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:13.506
In theory, it's very simple.

00:23:13.506 --> 00:23:16.090
But imagine all the things you
have to do to instrument all

00:23:16.090 --> 00:23:17.620
your code to be aware of this.

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:20.080
Well, in Spring
Cloud, we have an SPI

00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:21.810
called Spring Cloud Sleuth--

00:23:21.810 --> 00:23:24.580
sleuth, like a detective,
like a Sherlock Holmes.

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:26.825
Spring Cloud Sleuth is a
distributed tracing SPI.

00:23:26.825 --> 00:23:28.450
And there are different
implementations

00:23:28.450 --> 00:23:32.920
of which I think the Spring
Cloud, GCP, Google Cloud Stack

00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:33.790
Trace--

00:23:33.790 --> 00:23:36.850
Stackdriver Trace implementation
is one of the best.

00:23:36.850 --> 00:23:40.930
So we're going to use that
here for our application.

